Product overview
Product name: OneShot Video
Purpose: Allow a user to record/upload a short video, generate a one-time view link, and guarantee that the backend will serve it at most once, then delete it.

Core promise:

“This video can be viewed once via this link. After that, it’s gone from our system.”

2. Goals and non‑goals
Goals:

G1: Support upload of short video messages (e.g., ≤ 100 MB).

G2: Generate a one‑time token URL for each uploaded video.

G3: Enforce single‑view semantics at the backend.

G4: Delete video and metadata immediately after first successful view or after expiration.

Non‑goals:

NG1: Prevent screen recording or external capture.

NG2: Provide user accounts, auth, or contact lists.

NG3: Provide rich UI; initial version is minimal HTML or API‑only.

3. Primary user stories
As a sender, I want to upload a video and get back a one‑time link I can share.

As a recipient, I want to open the link, watch the video once, and understand that it won’t be available again.

As the system owner, I want videos and tokens to be deleted automatically after first view or after a time limit.

4. Functional requirements
4.1 Endpoints
1. POST /upload

Input:

Multipart form:

file: video file

Optional: expires_in_seconds (default e.g. 86400)

Behavior:

Validate file type/size.

Store file in configured storage (local or S3‑like).

Create DB record:

id

storage_path

token (random, URL‑safe)

viewed (bool, default false)

expires_at (now + expires_in_seconds)

created_at

Return JSON:

view_url: https://host/v/{token}

expires_at

2. GET /v/<token> (one‑view playback)

Behavior:

Look up record by token.

If not found → 404 “Message not found or already viewed.”

If expires_at < now → delete record + file, return 410 “Message expired.”

If viewed == true → 410 “Message already viewed.”

If valid:

Stream video to client (e.g., send_file/send_from_directory or signed URL redirect).

Mark viewed = true.

Trigger deletion:

Option A: delete file + record immediately after response is sent.

Option B: mark for deletion and let a background job clean up.

3. GET /status/<token> (optional)

Behavior:

Returns minimal status:

exists (bool)

viewed (bool)

expired (bool)

For debugging or sender UI; not required for v1.

5. Data model
Table: videos

id: UUID or integer PK

token: unique, random string (indexed)

storage_path: string (path or key in storage)

viewed: boolean

created_at: datetime

expires_at: datetime

Optional fields:

viewed_at: datetime

uploader_ip: string (for rate limiting / abuse detection)

6. Core flows
6.1 Upload flow
Sender hits POST /upload with video.

Backend:

Validates file.

Stores file.

Creates DB record with token + expiry.

Backend returns view_url to sender.

6.2 View flow (first view)
Recipient opens view_url.

Backend resolves token:

If invalid/expired/viewed → error page.

Else:

Streams video.

Sets viewed = true, viewed_at = now.

Deletes file + record (immediate or via job).

6.3 Expiration flow
Background job (e.g., cron) runs periodically:

Finds records where expires_at < now OR viewed = true and file_exists.

Deletes file from storage.

Deletes DB record (or marks as archived).

7. Non‑functional requirements
Security:

Tokens must be high‑entropy, unguessable.

No directory listing or direct file URLs without token.

Performance:

Optimized for small/medium videos; no need for adaptive streaming in v1.

Privacy:

No logging of video content.

Minimal metadata retention.

Reliability:

If deletion fails, system should retry; worst case, video becomes inaccessible via token but may linger in storage until cleanup.

8. Risks and constraints
Copy risk: Recipient can still capture video externally.

Network issues: If connection drops mid‑view, define behavior:

v1 simple rule: first successful response counts as “viewed,” even if user didn’t finish watching.

Storage cost: Need cleanup job to avoid orphaned files.
